            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbit"} qbit
            states of relevance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            — follow from the formal properties of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-compact+category"} dagger-compact category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+Hilbert+spaces"} finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces
          %p
            Conversely, much of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            can thus be formulated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            %em any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-compact+category"} dagger-compact category
          %p
            A transparent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            calculus in †-compact categories provides an intuitive and powerful tool for reasoning in
            = succeed "-compact" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
          %h2#quantum_mechanical_concepts_in_compact_categories
            Quantum mechanical concepts in
            = succeed "-compact" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo ,
                  %mo †
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,\otimes,I, \dagger)
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-compact+category"} †-compact category
          %p
            We list various concepts in quantum mechanics and their corresponding incarnation in terms of structures in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %h3#classical_measurement_outcomes Classical measurement outcomes
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable"} observable
            in quantum mechanics formulated on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            is modeled by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+operator"} self-adjoint operator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+basis"} orthogonal basis
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
          %p
            That, more abstractly, the notion of orthogonal basis of an object can be phrased intrinsically inside any suitable
            = succeed "-compact" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CoeckePavlovicVicary"} CoeckePavlovicVicary
          %h3#complex_phases Complex phases
          %p
            The underlying “algebra of quantum amplitudes” of the corresponding quantum mechanical system is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            of the tensor unit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi End
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{C}_C = End_C(I)
                  \\,.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Vicary"}> Vicary
            ) it is shown that in
            = succeed "-compact" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
            = succeed "-structure," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}_C
            has the properties that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                it is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                of characteristic 0 with involution
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo †
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
            %li
              %p
                the subfield
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
                fixed under
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo †
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
                is
                = succeed "able." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order"} order
          %p
            If furthermore every bounded sequence of measurements in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with values in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_C
            has a least upper bound, then it follows that this field coincides with the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+number"} complex number
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{C}_C = \mathbb{C}
          %p and moreover
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi C
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}_C = \mathbb{R}
                  \\,.
          %h3#ComplPosMaps Completely positive maps
          %p
            The behaviour of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+channel"} quantum channel
            %em completely positive maps
            has an elegant categorical description in terms of
            = succeed "-compact" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
            %a{:href => "#SelingerPositive"} Selinger
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#CoeckePositive"} Coecke
          %h2#quantum_logic Quantum logic
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} Symmetric monoidal categories
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-compact+categories"} †-compact categories
            have as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            a fragment of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            and as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            a flavor of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-compact+categories"} †-compact categories
            has an equivalent expression in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AbramskyDuncan05"} Abramsky-Duncan 05
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Duncan06"} Duncan 06
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            of quantum mechanics is the correct formalization of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"}> quantum logic
            ”. An exposition of this point of view is in (
            %a{:href => "#BaezStay09"}> Baez-Stay 09
            ).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} quantum circuit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+teleportation"} quantum teleportation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order-theoretic+structure+in+quantum+mechanics"} order-theoretic structure in quantum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"}> linear logic
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computing"} quantum computing
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/JBW-algebraic+quantum+mechanics"} JBW-algebraic quantum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+network"}> tensor network
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
          %h2#References References
          %div
            %h3#ReferencesQuantumInformationTheoryViaStringDiagrams Quantum information theory via String diagrams
            %h4#GeneralReferencesQuantumInformationTheoryViaStringDiagrams General
            %p
              The observation that a natural language for
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit+diagrams"} quantum circuit diagrams
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-compact+categories"} †-compact categories
              (see
              = succeed "):" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
            %ul
              %li#AbramskyCoecke04
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samson+Abramsky"}> Samson Abramsky
